{% doc %}
  Page guard. Include it, do not call it as a function: it ends the response
  with a redirect or a 403 and that only propagates through include.

  Anonymous visitors are sent to log in and come back. A logged in user
  without the permission gets a 403.

  @param {string} do - The permission the page requires
  @param {object} [entity] - The ticket the permission is checked against
{% enddoc %}
{% liquid
  function current_profile = 'modules/user/helpers/current_profile'
  function can = 'modules/support/helpers/can', do: do, entity: entity

  unless can
    if current_profile.user.id == null
      session return_to = context.location.href

      comment
        Set the flash with the user module's helper, which writes the
        {message, severity} shape its generated layout reads, and take the
        plain redirect_to tag. modules/core/helpers/redirect_to would be the
        obvious choice and is the wrong one here: it publishes the core
        {error, notice, info} shape over the same session key, which that
        layout reads as blank. The argument is a translation key, not a
        translated string, because the helper runs t itself.
      endcomment
      function _ = 'modules/user/helpers/flash', error: null, info: 'modules/support/authorization.redirect_anonymous_info', notice: null, force_clear: null
      redirect_to '/sessions/new'
      break
    else
      response_status 403
      # platformos-check-disable DeprecatedTag
      include 'modules/support/authorization/forbidden'
      # platformos-check-enable DeprecatedTag
      break
    endif
  endunless
%}
